 ## Image prompts
 - Anchor: "Use the woman from the character reference, wearing <outfit>." + where she stands, framing, props on the table with left/right **of the frame**, her action frozen mid-motion, "She looks straight into the camera, mouth open mid-sentence." Tail: "Natural candid iPhone 14 Pro Max photo, handheld, slight tilt. Vertical 9:16. Deep focus, entire frame sharp, no bokeh, no background blur. No readable store names or brand logos."
 - Delta: "Same exact image, except <what's gone> and <what's new>. <action>. <state of the container that received something>. She looks straight into the camera, mouth open mid-sentence." + "Same lighting as the reference image. Natural candid iPhone 14 Pro Max photo."
-- **Face always on camera**: every talking frame shows her face, chest up, looking into the lens, even when the original crops to hands (e.g. chin-down blender shots). Better for continuity and lip sync.
+- **Copy the original's shot, not just its action (student rule, 2026-10-28).** When the original cuts to a close-up (e.g. hands squashing a tomato over the counter, palm up toward the lens, juice dripping into a puddle), make that same close-up: same camera distance and height, same hand pose, same leaning, face out of frame if it is out of frame there. Put the original frame next to the generated image and compare before moving on; the first try at row 2's tomato shot came out too wide and needed a "tight close-up… hand fills the middle of the frame… face and kitchen out of frame" redo. This replaces the old "face always on camera" rule.
+- Talking frames where the original shows the face: her face, looking into the lens, mouth open mid-sentence. Close-ups without a face: her voice still carries the line (the clip prompt says she talks off camera).
 - **Location reference image in the box**: it copies that photo's framing and people. Say "Use only the room from the second reference image, not its framing and not its people" and spell the framing out explicitly (e.g. "camera at arm's length: head and shoulders fill the top half, hands the bottom half").
 - Continuity: the used item is put away / set down; the bowl shows everything poured so far.
 - Never describe the **subject's** face, hair, body or the lighting. Prop people DO get described.
